  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Logan Webb's eight innings of two-run ball was good enough to win any normal game, but they played this one instead.",
    "what_this_means": "Webb held Miami to five hits through eight innings while the Giants managed four hits and six strikeouts. They scored once, in the third, when Casey Schmitt drove in a run. That was the sum total of their offensive output. Webb pitched like a winner and got a loss because the lineup couldn't find anything after the first three innings. They had three runners in scoring position and converted one into a run. The other two at-bats produced nothing. This is where inconsistency lives. Not in the doing, but in the doing when it matters. Webb did his job perfectly. The offense went quiet and stayed there for six innings straight. You don't lose games like this by chance.",
    "what_to_watch": "Oakland's Aaron Civale carries a 4.91 ERA into tomorrow night's matchup, giving the Giants a clearer picture than they had yesterday.",
